1. The two functions reproduce both truth tables
   A      B      and(A, B)  or(A, B)
   true   true   true       true
   true   false  false      true
   false  true   false      true
   false  false  false      false

2. and() is not the same as &&: a function gets both arguments already evaluated
   noisy(left) && noisy(right):
     evaluated left
     = false
   and(noisy(left), noisy(right)):
     evaluated left
     evaluated right
     = false

3. Operators as trait method calls: true of & | !, not of && ||
   true & false = false  true.bitand(false) = false
   true | false = true   true.bitor(false)  = true
   !true        = false  true.not()         = false
   std::ops has no trait for && or || (use std::ops::And is E0432)

4. An immutable binding may be assigned once, not only at the let
   let a; then a = true; compiles without mut: a = true

5. Shadowing hides a name; the old value is untouched
   first still reads true, the new flag is false

6. A type can come from a later line, not only from the value
   before any push: alloc::vec::Vec<bool>
   after answers.push(true): [true]

7. A function with no -> returns (), and so does main
   nothing_to_return() = ()

8. The ! makes println a macro; the brackets can be any of three kinds
   parentheses: true AND false = false
   brackets:    true OR false = true
   braces:      false AND false = false
